    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1807</span><span class="rf-text">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and christened Joseph-Marie Garibaldi, into a Ligurian family in the coastal trade</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1834</span><span class="rf-text">Takes part in a failed Mazzinian insurrection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>; sentenced to death, he escapes and sails for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1842</span><span class="rf-text">Takes command of the Uruguayan fleet and raises the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, the Redshirts, for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war">Uruguayan Civil War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text">Commands the defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> against the French and retreats to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a>; his wife Anita dies near Comacchio</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1859</span><span class="rf-text">Leads the Hunters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> against the Austrians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence">Second Italian War of Independence</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1860</span><span class="rf-text">Leads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a>, conquers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and greets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum">Teano</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1862</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marches</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under the slogan &quot;Rome or Death&quot; and is wounded and captured by government troops at Aspromonte</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1882</span><span class="rf-text">Dies on Caprera; he is buried on his farm there rather than cremated as he had wished</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Giuseppe Garibaldi was born and christened Joseph-Marie Garibaldi on 4 July 1807 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, to the Ligurian family of Domenico Garibaldi from Chiavari and Maria Rosa Nicoletta Raimondi from Loano. In 1814 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> returned Nice to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-i-of-sardinia">Victor Emmanuel I of Sardinia</a>; in 1860 the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> would give it back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, over Garibaldi's objections. His family's involvement in coastal trade drew him to a life at sea.</p><p>From 1828 to 1832 he lived in the Pera district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, where he taught Italian, French and mathematics and had close ties with the Sardinian exile network in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. In April 1833 he sailed to Taganrog, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, aboard the schooner Clorinda with a shipment of oranges. There he met Giovanni Battista Cuneo from Oneglia, a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>'s secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a> movement, which sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> as a liberal republic through political and social reform.</p><p>In November 1833 Garibaldi met Mazzini in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the start of a long relationship that later became troubled. He joined the Carbonari revolutionary association, and in February 1834 he took part in a failed Mazzinian insurrection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. He was sentenced to death but escaped, and sailed by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beylik</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, where he spent 14 years in exile.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/anita-garibaldi">Anita Garibaldi<span>Married</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a> Garibaldi took up the cause of the Riograndense Republic, joining the rebels known as the Ragamuffins in the war of 1835. There he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anita-garibaldi">Ana Maria de Jesus Ribeiro</a> da Silva, known as Anita, who fought beside him at Imbituba and Laguna in 1839. In 1841 the couple moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, where he worked as a trader and schoolmaster; they married the following year. In 1842 he took command of the Uruguayan fleet and raised an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war">Uruguayan Civil War</a> from the city's large Italian population: 4,205 of 30,000 inhabitants in an 1843 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera">Fructuoso Rivera</a> against Manuel Oribe's Blancos and the Argentine forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a>, he defended Montevideo between 1842 and 1848 and won two victories in 1846 with amphibious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> brought him home with about 60 legionaries. Rebuffed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert of Sardinia</a>, he fought for the provisional government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1849 he commanded the defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, defeating a far larger French army at the Porta San Pancrazio on 30 April before the French took the city after a siege. He withdrew with 4,000 troops, hunted by Austrian, French, Spanish and Neapolitan forces; only 250 men remained when he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a>, and Anita, carrying their fifth child, died near Comacchio during the retreat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A second exile took Garibaldi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, where he worked in Antonio Meucci's candle factory on Staten Island, and as a merchant captain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Tyneside. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1854, he bought half of the island of Caprera. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence">Second Italian War of Independence</a> broke out in 1859 he was appointed major general and formed the Hunters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, who beat the Austrians at Varese, Como and elsewhere. From then on he abandoned Mazzini's republican ideal, judging that only the Sardinian monarchy could free Italy.</p><p>In April 1860 uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> gave him his chance. With the Thousand he sailed from Quarto, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, on 5 May and landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsala">Marsala</a> on 11 May. At Calatafimi on 15 May he led 800 volunteers to victory over 1,500 with an uphill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> charge, and the next day he declared himself dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>. Palermo surrendered after a siege and a truce arranged by a British admiral, and he won the hard Battle of Milazzo.</p><p>Crossing the Strait of Messina, he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> by train on 7 September. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a> on 30 September his 24,000 volunteers could not decisively defeat about 25,000 Neapolitans; his largest battle was settled by the arrival of the Royal Sardinian Army. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum">Teano</a> on 26 October he greeted Victor Emmanuel as King of Italy, and he retired to Caprera, refusing any reward. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> was created on 17 March 1861.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1845, during the Anglo-French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, Garibaldi led what are remembered as the infamous sacks of Martín García island and Gualeguaychú. Of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1860, historians Clough et al. argue that his volunteers were students, artisans and professionals rather than peasants; the peasants who backed him acted out of hatred of exploitive landlords and Neapolitan officials, and Garibaldi, who had no interest in social revolution, sided with the landlords against the rioting peasants.</p><p>His chief rival on his own side was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour</a>, whose realpolitik he mistrusted and whom he attacked in parliament in April 1860 for ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>. Cavour in turn feared that Garibaldi would invade the Papal States and bring French intervention. Garibaldi was intensely anti-Catholic and anti-papal; Catholics around the world viewed his hostility to the pope's temporal power with distrust, and he was excommunicated. In 1862 he raised volunteers under the slogan "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> or Death" against the government's wishes. On 28 August regular troops met them at Aspromonte; Garibaldi forbade his men to return fire, and he was wounded in the foot and imprisoned.</p><p>In 1867 he told the League of Peace and Freedom that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> "ought to be abolished", and in 1875 he urged "the oppressed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>" to drive "the Turk" back to Broussa. Denis Mack Smith judged that socialism meant nothing very revolutionary to him, and that he perhaps flaunted the word partly to shock the Mazzinians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1866, with the full support of the Italian government, Garibaldi led some 40,000 Hunters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> and defeated the Austrians at Bezzecca. After the regular forces' defeats at Custoza and Lissa, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, largely because of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s successes, and he was ordered to halt short of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a>. He replied by telegram: Obbedisco! ("I obey"). In 1867 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> again; the Papal army, supported by a French force, defeated his badly armed volunteers at Mentana, where he was shot in the leg, and the government imprisoned him once more.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-empire">Second French Empire</a> collapsed in 1870 he took the side of the new Government of National Defense, commanded the volunteer Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and was elected to the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>. In 1871 he declared his full support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> and called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a> "the sun of the future". Elected again to the Italian parliament, he spent much of his late years on Caprera; in 1879 he founded the League of Democracy with Felice Cavallotti, Alberto Mario and Agostino Bertani, for universal suffrage and the emancipation of women.</p><p>Ill and confined to bed by arthritis, he married Francesca Armosino, with whom he already had three children, in 1880. He died on Caprera on 2 June 1882, his bed moved so that he could see the sea. His wishes for a simple funeral and cremation were not respected.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Garibaldi's popularity, his skill at rousing the common people and his military exploits are credited with making the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> possible. He is counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> and Mazzini among Italy's "fathers of the fatherland", and twentieth-century historiography and popular culture regard him as Italy's greatest national hero. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> still celebrates him as a contributor to its reconstitution.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> were among those who praised him, and he inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Che Guevara</a>. A. J. P. Taylor called him "the only wholly admirable figure in modern history"; Denis Mack Smith wrote that at the height of his glory he was perhaps the most famous person in Italy. G. M. Trevelyan's trilogy of 1907–11 portrayed him, in David Cannadine's words, as a Carlylean hero whose inspired leadership created the Italian nation. His religious convictions are unclear to historians: in 1882 he wrote that "Man created God, not God created Man", yet his autobiography has him call himself a good Christian.</p><p>Five ships of the Italian Navy, a Polish legion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a> and a French legion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> took his name, and statues of him stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and elsewhere. In 2012 his descendants announced plans to exhume his remains for DNA analysis; they were sidelined in 2013.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani<span>1847</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Giuseppe Garibaldi (1807–1882), a general and republican from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, fought in the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> in 1860 won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>, and he is counted among Italy's &quot;fathers of the fatherland&quot;, though his marches on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> failed.</em></p>

